<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Iolo Morganwg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1747">1747</a>–<a href="/years/1826">1826</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Edward Williams</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edward Williams, better known by his bardic name Iolo Morganwg ('Iolo of Glamorgan'), was a Welsh antiquarian, poet and collector, born near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llancarfan">Llancarfan</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vale-of-glamorgan">Vale of Glamorgan</a>. He was seen as an expert collector of medieval Welsh literature, but after his death it emerged that he had forged several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, among them poems he attributed to Dafydd ap Gwilym in 1789 and the third series of Welsh Triads in The Myvyrian Archaiology of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> (1801–1807). In 1792 he founded the Gorsedd, a community of Welsh bards, at Primrose Hill in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, through which he co-opted the Eisteddfod revival. The philosophy of his forgeries has had an enormous impact on neo-Druidism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/iolo-morganwg.jpg" alt="Iolo Morganwg" width="330" height="348" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Edward_Williams,_bardd_braint_a_defod_(4672175).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In Wales the bardic order, revived and codified by the poet and forger <b>Iolo Morganwg</b>, lives on in the eisteddfods.</blockquote><a href="/religion/celtic-religion">Celtic religion · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>James Macpherson’s poems of Ossian, published in the 1760s as translations of ancient Gaelic verse but largely his own, were read across Europe; <b>Iolo Morganwg</b> devised the bardic Gorsedd in 1792; and national movements in Ireland, Scotland, Wales and Brittany looked to the Celts as ancestors.</blockquote><a href="/culture/celts">Celts · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llancarfan">Llancarfan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Flemingston, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.42,-3.37&amp;year=1798" aria-label="Llancarfan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4474 383 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Iolo Morganwg"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4924 653h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4924 653h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.42,-3.37&amp;year=1798">What was here in 1798? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
